<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikhail Frunze</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1925">1925</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikhail <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Frunze</a> was a Bolshevik revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> commander. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> he defeated Kolchak's forces in the Urals, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 1920 and drove Wrangel's army from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> later that year. He succeeded Trotsky as People's Commissar for Military and Naval Affairs in 1925 and died after surgery that year; the city of Bishkek bore his name from 1926 to 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-frunze.jpg" alt="Mikhail Frunze" width="320" height="424" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michai%C5%82_Frunze_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
